<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Helena of Adiabene</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. c. 50 CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Helena was queen of Adiabene in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> in the 1st century CE, wife of King Monobazus I and mother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/izates-ii">Izates II</a>. According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josephus">Josephus</a> she and her son converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaism">Judaism</a>. She settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>, sent food to the city during a famine in the 40s CE, and was buried in the monumental tomb now called the Tomb of the Kings.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/helena-of-adiabene.jpg" alt="Helena of Adiabene" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hanay · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Helena_of_Adiabene_Sarcophagus_1.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
